I’d keep the current design: the API asks Batch directly while the task is active, and reads Blob once it’s done. Timefold doesn’t need to write its status to Blob during the run, and this doesn’t conflict with Sigma-I.

The Sigma-I design already works this way. In documents/sigmaI §8.3, the Bridge writes its status every 10 s to the file on the node (wd/status/{runId}.json), not to Blob. Blob gets the status only once, when the task ends, through the same outputFiles upload Timefold uses. While the task runs, the API reads the live file the same way it does for Timefold. So both engines follow one rule. The only difference is which Batch job the API asks (BATCH_JOB_ID or BATCH_JOB_ID_SIGMAI).

The idea that the Bridge writes Blob continuously comes from two loosely worded places in the Sigma-I docs. The Summary says “keeps status/{runId}.json up to date”, and the JP plan’s diagram shows BR->>BLOB: status = Completed. That arrow is really Batch’s end-of-task upload. Those two spots should be reworded; I haven’t edited the Sigma-I docs.

About the “write only once” limit: that limit is on outputFiles, not on Blob. A script inside the task can write Blob as often as it wants by calling Blob itself with the pool identity’s token. I wouldn’t do that yet. It needs curl and a token inside the container, adds a new way to fail mid-solve, and puts three writers on one file. If you ever need it, do it the same way in both engines and the API rule stays the same.

Gaps worth closing in the API (Sigma-I design §7 already has these; Timefold doesn’t yet):

Task ends but no final status reaches Blob (upload error, timeout, lost node). Today /status says Submitted forever. Fix: if Batch says the task finished more than 2 minutes ago, return Failed.
Creating the Batch task fails after Submitted was written. Fix: write Failed (SubmitFailed) in that case.
Errors are swallowed. The live-read code drops errors without logging them, so a broken read looks like “still Submitted”. Log anything other than 404.

